export async function generateMetadata({ params }: PageProps<"/products/[slug]">) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const product = await getProduct(slug);
  return { title: product ? `${product.name} | Trailhead` : "Not found | Trailhead" };
}

steps.body:
Some data depends on who's asking: their cart, their name, the filter they picked. To show it, the server has to read the request itself, its cookies, its headers, and its URL. Those reads are cheap, but they change how Next.js can render the page, so they're worth understanding before you sprinkle them around.

## cookies and headers

`cookies()` and `headers()` come from `next/headers`, and both are async in Next.js 16, so you `await` them. `(await cookies()).get("th_session")?.value` reads the session cookie that the Trailhead API's login set, recalled from the React course's lesson on calling APIs. `(await headers()).get("accept-language")` reads a request header. In Server Components they're read-only. Setting a cookie belongs to Server Actions and Route Handlers, which later lessons cover.

In Trailhead, `apiFetch` does this for you: with `{ session: true }`, it reads the `th_session` cookie and sends it along to the API, so the API knows who's asking. Any function that passes that option is reading the request.

## They make the part that reads them dynamic

Recall that with Cache Components, Next.js renders as much of each page as it can ahead of time, before any request. A cookie doesn't exist ahead of time, so any component that reads one can't be part of that early render. It has to render when the request arrives, which is called **dynamic** rendering. The same goes for `headers()`, for `searchParams`, and for `params` values that weren't listed ahead of time.

Next.js makes you say where that boundary is. Read request data outside a `Suspense` boundary, and the build stops with the error you met in the routing lesson. Read it inside one, and only that part waits for the request, while the rest of the page is prerendered and sent at once. That's why the account page below reads the session inside `Suspense`, and why the next lesson can explain what the build does with everything outside it.

## searchParams as URL state

The React course put filters in the URL, so they survive a reload and can be shared. In Next.js, a page reads them from its `searchParams` prop, a promise like `params`. `/products?category=tents` gives `{ category: "tents" }`. A value can also be an array, for `?category=tents&category=packs`, or missing, so check its type before using it. Changing the search string with a `Link` renders the page again on the server with the new values, and a client component can read the same values with `useSearchParams`, as the routing lesson showed.

## connection() for everything else that's per request

Some values change on every request without reading the request at all: the current time, a random number, a fresh ID. Next.js refuses to bake one of those into a prerendered page, since everyone would see the value from the build. Use `new Date()` while rendering, and the build stops with an error that offers fixes. The one for per-request values is `await connection()`, from `next/server`. It reads nothing, but it tells Next.js that what follows belongs to the request, like reading a cookie would. Put it at the top of the component, inside a `Suspense` boundary like any other request data:

```tsx sketch
import { connection } from "next/server";

async function ShippingNote() {
  await connection();
  const hour = new Date().getUTCHours();
  return <p>{hour < 21 ? "Ships today" : "Ships tomorrow"}</p>;
}
```

The next lesson explains what happens to everything outside those boundaries, and why it's worth keeping them small.

